WEBVTT

00:00:11.800 --> 00:00:29.800
<v Ira Glass>There's a new film out that's in festivals called The Boys and the Bees, just won a prize at Sundance. It's about a real family with two sons. The dad's a beekeeper in Georgia. And I talked to the filmmaker, Arielle Knight. As she pointed out, the two boys are very different. Carver's six. Aaro's four.
<v Ira Glass>有一部名为《蜜蜂男孩》（The Boys and the Bees）的新纪录片正在各大电影节上映，刚刚在圣丹斯电影节上获了奖。它讲述了一个真实的家庭，家里有两个儿子。爸爸在佐治亚州当养蜂人。我采访了这部片的导演阿里尔·奈特（Arielle Knight）。正如她所指出的，这两个男孩非常不同。卡弗（Carver）六岁，阿罗（Aaro）四岁。

00:00:29.800 --> 00:00:43.240
<v Arielle Knight>Aaro is the youngest. And he's kind of always bouncing around. He's comedic relief in the film. And Carver is the oldest. And he's a lot more serious. He's a lot more thoughtful.
<v Arielle Knight>阿罗是老小。他总是精力充沛、活蹦乱跳的。他在片中负责搞笑。卡弗是老大。他严肃得多，也更有深思熟虑的感觉。

00:00:43.240 --> 00:00:52.800
<v Ira Glass>Carver's the one who reminds his little brother what the rules are. Aaro just breezes past them. And the way all this plays out in this family of beekeepers?
<v Ira Glass>卡弗总是提醒弟弟规则是什么。而阿罗总是直接无视它们。这一切在这个养蜂家庭里是如何上演的呢？

00:00:52.800 --> 00:00:58.960
<v Arielle Knight>Aaro's been stung many, many times by bees, and Carver has never been stung.
<v Arielle Knight>阿罗被蜜蜂蛰过很多很多次，而卡弗从来没被蛰过。

00:00:58.960 --> 00:01:01.280
<v Ira Glass>And how do you explain that?
<v Ira Glass>你对此怎么解释？

00:01:01.280 --> 00:01:09.280
<v Arielle Knight>[LAUGHS] I mean, Carver is probably always paying attention, taking his time, moving slowly--
<v Arielle Knight>[笑声] 我的意思是，卡弗可能一直都很专心、不慌不忙、行动迟缓——

00:01:09.280 --> 00:01:10.340
<v Ira Glass>Rules follower.
<v Ira Glass>一个遵守规则的人。

00:01:10.340 --> 00:01:21.570
<v Arielle Knight>Exactly. And Aaro just sort of puts on his suit too fast, and there's a bee in there, or he steps somewhere where he shouldn't, or he's just-- you know.
<v Arielle Knight>没错。而阿罗穿防护服总是太快了，结果里面藏了一只蜜蜂，或者他踩到了不该踩的地方，或者他就是——你知道的。

00:01:21.570 --> 00:01:38.910
<v Ira Glass>They have parents who have made a very deliberate choice to spend a ton of time with their kids, birdwatching and fishing and going through the woods and lifting up rocks to see what's underneath them, explaining everything about nature. And there's a scene that Arielle captured on film of the boys with their dad, Nehemiah.
<v Ira Glass>他们的父母做出了一种非常刻意的选择，要花大量时间陪孩子：观察鸟类、钓鱼、在树林里穿梭、掀开石头看看下面有什么，并向他们解释大自然的一切。片中有一个阿里尔拍下的场景，记录了两个男孩和他们的爸爸尼米亚（Nehemiah）在一起的画面。

00:01:38.910 --> 00:01:55.540
<v Arielle Knight>So we were wrapping up filming for the day. And Nehemiah, usually in the evening, will check on the bees. So he's checking on the bees. He's lifting the racks of beehives out of their boxes. And then he gets stung.
<v Arielle Knight>当时我们正在收工，准备结束一天的拍摄。尼米亚通常会在傍晚查看蜜蜂的情况。于是他去查看蜜蜂。他把蜂箱里的蜂架从箱子里提出来。然后他就被蛰了。

00:01:55.540 --> 00:02:06.310
<v Nehemiah>Ouch. Ah, it got me. You boys back up, though. You all don't have a suit on, and it just stung me. So I don't want you to get stung.
<v Nehemiah>哎哟。啊，蛰到我了。不过你们两个往后退。你们没穿防护服，它刚蛰了我。所以我可不想你们被蛰。

00:02:06.310 --> 00:02:10.110
<v Carver>But what does it feel like? It hurts so bad?
<v Carver>但那是什么感觉？真的很疼吗？

00:02:10.110 --> 00:02:12.070
<v Nehemiah>I mean, you can handle it.
<v Nehemiah>我的意思是，你能应付得来。

00:02:12.070 --> 00:02:21.450
<v Arielle Knight>Carver creeps up to his dad and says something that I couldn't hear in the moment but that Nehemiah repeats. And he says--
<v Arielle Knight>卡弗悄悄走到爸爸身边，说了一句我当时没听清但尼米亚重复了一遍的话。他说——

00:02:21.450 --> 00:02:22.590
<v Nehemiah>You want to get stung?
<v Nehemiah>你想被蛰一下吗？

00:02:22.590 --> 00:02:29.110
<v Carver>Yeah. Everybody in my family got stung except for me.
<v Carver>对啊。家里每个人都被蛰了，就我没有。

00:02:29.110 --> 00:02:35.710
<v Nehemiah>I don't know if Mommy would want you to get intentionally stung. It kind of hurts, boy, it kind of hurts.
<v Nehemiah>我不知道妈妈会不会希望你故意去挨蛰。这挺疼的，孩子，这可挺疼的。

00:02:35.710 --> 00:02:37.110
<v Carver>I know that hurts.
<v Carver>我知道这很疼。

00:02:37.110 --> 00:02:38.870
<v Ira Glass>I know that hurts, Carver says.
<v Ira Glass>我知道这很疼，卡弗说。

00:02:38.870 --> 00:02:42.710
<v Nehemiah>I can get the bee and put it on you, and it can sting you.
<v Nehemiah>我可以把蜜蜂抓来放到你身上，让它蛰你。

00:02:42.710 --> 00:02:46.790
<v Ira Glass>OK. Can we just talk about that choice that he's making there?
<v Ira Glass>好吧。我们能聊聊他当时做出的这个选择吗？

00:02:46.790 --> 00:03:03.370
<v Arielle Knight>So Nehemiah has said multiple times that, growing up, he'd always wished that the adults around him had been more curious about the things that he was curious about, the kinds of adventures that he was interested in getting into as a kid.
<v Arielle Knight>尼米亚曾多次说过，在自己成长过程中，他总是希望周围的成年人能对他的好奇心、以及他小时候想去经历的那种冒险表现出更多的好奇。

00:03:03.370 --> 00:03:06.410
<v Ira Glass>And he also seems very awake to these little teachable moments.
<v Ira Glass>而且他也显得非常敏锐，善于抓住这些微小的教育契机。

00:03:06.410 --> 00:03:19.230
<v Arielle Knight>He's a total teacher. And he's always kind of trying to find the edge in helping his kids navigate things that are scary to them or that they never tried before that they're curious about.
<v Arielle Knight>他完全是个天生的老师。他总是试图在边缘试探，帮助孩子们克服那些让他们感到害怕的、或者他们从未尝试过却又很好奇的事情。

00:03:19.230 --> 00:03:27.110
<v Nehemiah>I can get the bee and put it on you, and it can sting you. All right. Well, let's do it.
<v Nehemiah>我可以把蜜蜂抓来放到你身上，让它蛰你。好吧。那我们就来试试。

00:03:27.110 --> 00:03:33.150
<v Aaro>Mommy got stung. I got stung. Daddy got stung. You haven't got stung.
<v Aaro>妈妈被蛰过。我被蛰过。爸爸被蛰过。你还没被蛰过。

00:03:33.150 --> 00:03:35.030
<v Arielle Knight>So that's Aaro in the background.
<v Arielle Knight>背景里那是阿罗的声音。

00:03:35.030 --> 00:03:36.790
<v Ira Glass>Then Carver goes to his dad.
<v Ira Glass>然后卡弗走向他的爸爸。

00:03:36.790 --> 00:03:39.470
<v Carver>I just don't want to get stung in my face.
<v Carver>我只是不想被蛰到脸。

00:03:39.470 --> 00:03:44.230
<v Nehemiah>Yeah. Me either. That's the worst. We'll let you get stung right here.
<v Nehemiah>对。我也不想。那是（被蛰）最惨的地方。我们让你在这里被蛰吧。

00:03:44.230 --> 00:03:47.050
<v Arielle Knight>So Nehemiah is pointing to Carver's forearm.
<v Arielle Knight>尼米亚指着卡弗的前臂。

00:03:47.050 --> 00:03:49.890
<v Nehemiah>I'm telling you right now, son-- look at Daddy. It's going to hurt.
<v Nehemiah>我现在就告诉你，儿子——看着爸爸。这会很疼的。

00:03:49.890 --> 00:03:50.390
<v Carver>OK.
<v Carver>好吧。

00:03:50.150 --> 00:03:53.510
<v Nehemiah>OK? You sure you want to do it?
<v Nehemiah>好吧？你确定要这么做吗？

00:03:53.510 --> 00:03:54.150
<v Carver>Yeah.
<v Carver>嗯。

00:03:54.150 --> 00:03:56.030
<v Nehemiah>OK, my boy. I like it.
<v Nehemiah>好吧，我的好孩子。我喜欢这样。

00:03:56.030 --> 00:04:04.000
<v Arielle Knight>Carver is starting to look a little bit concerned right now. And he gives his dad a really weak high five.
<v Arielle Knight>卡弗现在看起来有点担忧了。他给爸爸击了个非常无力的掌。

00:04:04.000 --> 00:04:15.870
<v Nehemiah>Uh-uh, Black man dab. Yeah, there we go. I like it. All right. Let's do it. Which lucky girl are we going to choose for my son?
<v Nehemiah>不对，用黑人击掌手势。对，就是这样。我喜欢。好了。我们开始吧。我们要为我儿子挑选哪位幸运的姑娘呢？

00:04:15.870 --> 00:04:21.550
<v Ira Glass>Oh my god. So Carver's kind of touching his arm where he's about to get stung. His dad goes off to get a bee.
<v Ira Glass>天哪。卡弗正摸着他即将被蛰的手臂。他爸爸去抓蜜蜂了。

00:04:21.550 --> 00:04:25.330
<v Nehemiah>You ready? Tell me when you're ready. When you lift your arm up, then you'll be ready.
<v Nehemiah>准备好了吗？准备好了就告诉我。当你把手臂抬起来的时候，你就是准备好了。

00:04:25.330 --> 00:04:31.030
<v Arielle Knight>So Nehemiah is holding a bee by its wings with the stinger pointed out.
<v Arielle Knight>尼米亚正捏着一只蜜蜂的翅膀，把毒刺露在外面。

00:04:31.030 --> 00:04:38.190
<v Ira Glass>And then Carver has his arm kind of down, and his other hand is kind of, like, shielding it from his dad and the bee.
<v Ira Glass>卡弗把手臂垂在下面，另一只手挡在爸爸和蜜蜂之间。

00:04:38.190 --> 00:04:43.910
<v Arielle Knight>And he keeps kind of wincing and jerking his arm away in anticipation.
<v Arielle Knight>他不停地畏缩，出于本能把手臂往回缩。

00:04:43.910 --> 00:05:01.900
<v Nehemiah>You ready? Good job. All right. It's all right. Three-- don't jump. Don't jump. Don't jump. And I'm going to get it out, OK? I'm going to get it out. You sure you want to, son? You're acting like you don't want to now. I know you're nervous.
<v Nehemiah>准备好了吗？干得好。好了。没关系的。三——别跳。别跳。别跳。我会把它弄出来的，好吗？我会把它弄出来的。你确定要这么做吗，儿子？你现在的表现看起来像是不想了。我知道你很紧张。

00:05:01.900 --> 00:05:03.340
<v Carver>Just a little bit.
<v Carver>就一点点。

00:05:03.340 --> 00:05:14.140
<v Nehemiah>All right. Three. Relax, relax. Son, you can't do that. You just have to let it happen, OK?
<v Nehemiah>好吧。三。放松，放松。儿子，你不能这样。你必须顺其自然，好吗？

00:05:14.140 --> 00:05:16.140
<v Carver>Something.
<v Carver>什么东西。

00:05:16.140 --> 00:05:19.320
<v Ira Glass>It's so funny. He puts the bee on him, and the bee just won't do it.
<v Ira Glass>太搞笑了。他把蜜蜂放在他身上，可蜜蜂就是不肯蛰他。

00:05:19.320 --> 00:05:31.740
<v Nehemiah>Well, she doesn't want to sting you, eh? Well, she didn't want to get-- all right. Let me get another one. Dang, you just not a mean bee, huh?
<v Nehemiah>好吧，她不想蛰你，是吧？好吧，她不想——好吧。让我再抓一只。该死，你可真不是一只凶恶的蜜蜂，对吧？

00:05:31.740 --> 00:05:36.340
<v Aaro>You're just a nice, kind bee. You don't want anybody to get stung.
<v Aaro>你只是一只善良温和的蜜蜂。你不想让任何人被蛰。

00:05:36.340 --> 00:05:39.100
<v Arielle Knight>That's the younger brother again, talking to the bee.
<v Arielle Knight>又是弟弟在跟蜜蜂说话。

00:05:39.100 --> 00:05:40.900
<v Nehemiah>All right. Here we go.
<v Nehemiah>好啦。我们开始吧。

00:05:40.900 --> 00:05:43.180
<v Carver>Let's see.
<v Carver>让我看看。

00:05:43.180 --> 00:05:47.550
<v Nehemiah>Why are you playing with my son?
<v Nehemiah>你为什么在跟我儿子玩？

00:05:47.550 --> 00:05:48.900
<v Carver>[SCREAMS]
<v Carver>[尖叫]

00:05:48.900 --> 00:05:57.640
<v Nehemiah>OK. OK, OK, let me see. OK. Oh. See? Now let's get it out. Let's get it out. Now, see? Oh.
<v Nehemiah>好吧。好吧，好吧，让我看看。好吧。哦。看见没？现在我们把它拔出来。把它拔出来。现在，看到了吗？哦。

00:05:57.640 --> 00:05:58.660
<v Carver>[CRYING]
<v Carver>[哭泣]

00:05:58.660 --> 00:06:01.220
<v Nehemiah>Son, now you're making me feel bad.
<v Nehemiah>儿子，你现在让我觉得挺内疚的。

00:06:01.220 --> 00:06:16.400
<v Arielle Knight>So Nehemiah kind of looks at me in this moment, and I can see that there's a little bit of doubt in his eyes. He's kind of looking at me like, oh, man, I don't know if I did the right thing here. But then you can see the moment he also snaps back into dad mode.
<v Arielle Knight>这一刻尼米亚看了我一眼，我能看出他眼里有一丝犹豫。他看着我的眼神仿佛在说，哦，天哪，我不知道我刚才这么做到底对不对。但随即你就能看到他又回到了父亲的角色中。

00:06:16.400 --> 00:06:17.520
<v Nehemiah>Come here. Let me see.
<v Nehemiah>过来。让我看看。

00:06:17.520 --> 00:06:18.740
<v Carver>[CRYING]
<v Carver>[哭泣]

00:06:18.740 --> 00:06:31.680
<v Nehemiah>All right. Now wipe your tears off. Wipe your tears. Let me see it. Let me see it. Let me see it. Let me see it. Let me see it. Where is it at? Where was it?
<v Nehemiah>好了。现在把眼泪擦掉。擦掉眼泪。让我看看。让我看看。让我看看。让我看看。让我看看。它在哪儿？刚才在哪儿？

00:06:31.680 --> 00:06:33.100
<v Carver>[CRYING]
<v Carver>[哭泣]

00:06:33.100 --> 00:06:39.660
<v Nehemiah>OK. I got it out. OK. Good, good. Good, good, good.
<v Nehemiah>好了。我把它拔出来了。好吧。很好，很好。很好，很好，很好。

00:06:39.660 --> 00:06:45.700
<v Arielle Knight>Nehemiah is kneeling in front of Carver, and he's kind of examining the bee sting and wiping his tears away.
<v Arielle Knight>尼米亚跪在卡弗面前，仔细检查着蜂蛰的伤口，并帮他擦去眼泪。

00:06:45.700 --> 00:06:54.970
<v Nehemiah>Beekeepers get stung, right? You got your first one, my boy. High five? Now let me see where it is. Now let me see.
<v Nehemiah>养蜂人都会被蛰，对吧？你拿到你的第一次了，我的好孩子。击个掌？现在让我看看它在哪儿。现在让我看看。

00:06:54.970 --> 00:06:58.730
<v Carver>[WHIMPERING] It hurts.
<v Carver>[呜咽] 好疼。

00:06:58.730 --> 00:07:03.030
<v Nehemiah>I got it out, I promise. You want to make sure? Yeah, I got it out. Now--
<v Nehemiah>我把它拔出来了，我保证。你想确认一下吗？对，我把它拔出来了。现在——

00:07:03.030 --> 00:07:04.610
<v Carver>[INAUDIBLE]
<v Carver>[听不清]

00:07:04.610 --> 00:07:07.750
<v Nehemiah>Yeah, that hurts? Do you wish you didn't do it?
<v Nehemiah>对，很疼吗？你后悔这么做了吗？

00:07:07.750 --> 00:07:12.530
<v Carver>[CRYING] I still feel a little proud of myself.
<v Carver>[哭泣] 我还是觉得有点为自己骄傲。

00:07:12.530 --> 00:07:17.210
<v Nehemiah>I can't hear you. Huh? You said what?
<v Nehemiah>我听不见。啊？你刚说啥？

00:07:17.210 --> 00:07:20.270
<v Carver>I still feel a little proud of myself.
<v Carver>我还是觉得有点为自己骄傲。

00:07:20.270 --> 00:07:23.770
<v Nehemiah>You feel proud of yourself? Boy, I'm proud of you.
<v Nehemiah>你为自己感到骄傲？孩子，我为你骄傲。

00:07:23.770 --> 00:07:38.810
<v Ira Glass>Can I say, I just really love how Carver, after he gets stung, is just not totally sure that he's glad he did it? And then he kind of, like, sits there, and it stews in his brain for a couple seconds. And then he decides I'm going to declare victory.
<v Ira Glass>我能说吗，我真的很喜欢卡弗在被蛰之后，并不是完全确定自己很高兴这么做了？然后他坐在那儿，在脑子里琢磨了几秒钟。接着他决定：我要宣布胜利。

00:07:38.810 --> 00:07:51.370
<v Nehemiah>You should feel proud of yourself for that. I've never met any kid-- I've never met any kid that said, my whole family's been stung, and I haven't. I want to get-- look at mine. You see Daddy's right there?
<v Nehemiah>你真该为自己感到骄傲。我从来没见过哪个孩子——我从来没见过哪个孩子说，我全家都被蛰了，就我没被蛰。我想去——看我的。你看到爸爸这里也有一个了吗？

00:07:51.370 --> 00:08:17.970
<v Arielle Knight>So the rest of the evening-- they go on to have dinner. Carver keeps glancing down at his bee sting. Sometimes he gives it a little stroke. And it's kind of starting to swell up a bit at this point. But he's kind of beaming at it. He's a real beekeeper now. And like his dad said, beekeepers get stung.
<v Arielle Knight>接下来的整个傍晚——他们继续吃晚饭。卡弗不停地低头看着他的蜂蛰伤口。有时他轻轻抚摸它。此时伤口开始有点肿胀了。但他正对着伤口喜笑颜开。他现在是个真正的养蜂人了。就像他爸爸说的，养蜂人都会被蛰。

00:08:17.970 --> 00:08:23.650
<v Ira Glass>When you decide to do something that other people do not do, it makes you feel big.
<v Ira Glass>当你决定去做一件别人都不去做的事时，这会让你觉得自己很了不起。

00:08:23.650 --> 00:08:57.570
<v Ira Glass>Today on our program, we have people choosing to do things that most of us-- not only will we not do these things, most of us actively avoid these things the way that we avoid bee stings. We have stories today of oysters, of love. We have a courtroom drama. From WBEZ Chicago, it's This American Life. I'm Ira Glass. Stay with us.
<v Ira Glass>今天我们的节目将为大家讲述一些人的选择，他们做的事——不仅我们大多数人不会去做，而且我们绝大多数人会像躲避蜂蛰一样极力避开这些事。今天我们有关于生蚝的故事，有关于爱的故事。还有一出法庭戏。来自芝加哥WBEZ电台，这里是《美国生活》。我是艾拉·格拉斯（Ira Glass）。请继续收听。

00:08:57.570 --> 00:09:02.170
<v Ira Glass>It's This American Life, "Act One, Lessons in Shellfishness."
<v Ira Glass>这里是《美国生活》，第一幕：“贝类情结的教训”。

00:09:02.170 --> 00:09:15.330
<v Ira Glass>So John Tothill has been thinking a lot about people who make personal life choices that are different from most. He did a whole show inspired by a man like that, a show where he talks about that man and tells a personal story also. Tothill's a comedian. He's a British comedian.
<v Ira Glass>约翰·托希尔（John Tothill）一直在思考那些做出与大多数人不同的人生选择的人。他的一整场演出就是受这样一个男人的启发，在那场演出中，他谈到了那个男人，并讲述了一个个人故事。托希尔是个喜剧演员。他是个英国喜剧演员。

00:09:15.330 --> 00:09:40.050
<v Ira Glass>Quick note before he starts-- he refers to two things in this story that not everybody knows about here in the United States. He mentions the Edinburgh Fringe Festival. That's this big three-week-long performance festival in Europe where he happened to do this show. And he refers to Rustlers burgers, which Brits, I guess, know about. They are microwavable, budget-priced, ready-made frozen burgers on buns. Here's John Tothill.
<v Ira Glass>在他开始之前有个小提示——他在这个故事中提到了两件事，在美国的大家可能并不是都知道。他提到了爱丁堡艺穗节（Edinburgh Fringe Festival）。这是欧洲一个为期三周的大型表演艺术节，他当时恰好在那里演出。他还提到了 Rustlers 汉堡，我想英国人大概都知道。那是微波炉加热的、预算价格的、现成的冷冻汉堡包。接下来是约翰·托希尔。

00:09:40.050 --> 00:09:43.590
<v John Tothill>Hello. How are you all doing. You all right?
<v John Tothill>大家好。你们最近怎么样。都还好吧？

00:09:43.590 --> 00:09:44.800
<v John Tothill>[CHEERING]
<v John Tothill>[欢呼声]

00:09:44.800 --> 00:10:14.740
<v John Tothill>It's so good to be here. And of course, we've got to talk about Edward Dando, haven't we? Do we know about Edward Dando? Edward Dando, the celebrated 19th-century oyster glutton, Edward Dando? How do I tell you about-- OK, right. You know how on Wikipedia, if you look something up on Wikipedia, Wikipedia has to purport to be objective, but like, to a fault, you know? Wikipedia will be like, oh, Adolf Hitler was an Austrian-born German politician. And you think, yeah, get on with it. Come on. Do you know what I mean?
<v John Tothill>很高兴来到这里。当然，我们得聊聊爱德华·丹多（Edward Dando），对吧？我们都知道爱德华·丹多吗？爱德华·丹多，这位19世纪著名的生蚝饕餮大客，爱德华·丹多？我该怎么跟你们讲呢——好吧，对。你知道在维基百科上，如果你在维基百科上查阅某样东西，维基百科必须标榜自己是客观的，但就像是客观得过了头，你知道吧？维基百科会说，哦，阿道夫·希特勒是一位出生于奥地利的德国政治家。然后你会想，行了，说重点吧。拜托。你知道我的意思吗？

00:10:14.740 --> 00:10:26.240
<v John Tothill>But if you look up Edward Dando on Wikipedia, Wikipedia says, Edward Dando was a British thief. And it's like, OK, cancel my appointments for the afternoon.
<v John Tothill>但如果你在维基百科上查爱德华·丹多，维基百科上写着：爱德华·丹多是一个英国小偷。这就好比是：好吧，那我得把下午的约会都取消了。

00:10:26.240 --> 00:10:28.120
<v John Tothill>[LAUGHTER]
<v John Tothill>[笑声]

00:10:28.120 --> 00:10:45.500
<v John Tothill>So Edward Dando was a kind of minor celebrity in about the 1800s, completely forgotten about now. But he was famous in his day for frequenting oyster restaurants, right? This was like, at the height of Victorian London's obsession with oysters. Oysters just came out of the Thames. Londoners couldn't get enough oysters.
<v John Tothill>所以爱德华·丹多大约在19世纪算是个小有名气的人物，现在已经被彻底遗忘了。但在他那个年代，他因为经常光顾生蚝餐厅而闻名，对吧？那正是维多利亚时代伦敦人对生蚝痴迷的顶峰时期。生蚝直接从泰晤士河里打捞上来。伦敦人对生蚝简直百吃不厌。

00:10:45.500 --> 00:11:10.440
<v John Tothill>Edward Dando would go to these oyster restaurants, and he would order dozens of oysters at a time-- I mean, like, 200, 300 oysters at a time. And then he would draw a crowd, and he would performatively eat all of them, along with loaves of bread and kegs of ale. And then when the bill arrived, Edward Dando would swear to God he thought it was free.
<v John Tothill>爱德华·丹多会去这些生蚝餐厅，一次点几十个生蚝——我是说，一次点200个、300个生蚝。然后他会吸引一大群人，当众吃光所有的生蚝，还要配上几条面包和好几桶麦酒。接着，当账单送来时，爱德华·丹多会向上帝发誓，他以为那是免费的。

00:11:10.440 --> 00:11:16.640
<v John Tothill>[LAUGHTER]
<v John Tothill>[笑声]

00:11:16.640 --> 00:11:48.120
<v John Tothill>He claimed in court not to know how restaurants worked. And in these magistrates courts, he would always insist on representing himself. And in his testimonies, he would always begin by saying, Your Worship, I was peckish. And I refuse to go hungry in a land of plenty. Of course, harsh 19th-century justice, he would be thrown into jail, often for months on end. When he was in jail, he was thrown into solitary confinement because he kept eating the rations of the other prisoners.
<v John Tothill>他在法庭上声称自己不知道餐厅是怎么运作的。而在这些地方法院里，他总是坚持自己进行辩护。在作证时，他总是以这句话开头：“法官大人，我当时有点饿了。我拒绝在一个物产丰富的国家挨饿。”当然，根据严厉的19世纪司法制度，他会被扔进监狱，通常一关就是好几个月。当他在监狱里时，他被关进单人牢房，因为他总是不停地偷吃其他囚犯的口粮。

00:11:48.120 --> 00:12:09.540
<v John Tothill>And then-- and this is the clincher-- Edward Dando, every single time, on the day of his release-- not the day after his release. On the day of his release, Edward Dando would walk from the prison gates to the nearest oyster restaurant, and he would do it again.
<v John Tothill>然后——这是最绝的——爱德华·丹多，每一次，都在他刑满释放的那一天——不是释放的第二天。就在他出狱的那天，爱德华·丹多会从监狱大门一路走到最近的生蚝餐厅，然后把这一切再重演一遍。

00:12:09.540 --> 00:12:11.160
<v John Tothill>[LAUGHTER]
<v John Tothill>[笑声]

00:12:11.160 --> 00:12:45.430
<v John Tothill>That is a man who is free. That is a man who knows himself. And I love the story of Edward Dando. Because for me, the story of Edward Dando reminds us of how much there is to be gluttonous for in the world, you know? I love living in the modern world. I adore it. I like the kind of modern food that other people pretend to hate. I eat things like Rustlers burgers. Do you know why? Because I love it. Like, I really love it. People go, what are you talking about? What do you love? All of it. But do you know what I mean? Like, drinking, I love it. But even, like, sitting.
<v John Tothill>那才是一个真正自由的人。那是一个真正了解自己的人。我很喜欢爱德华·丹多的故事。因为对我而言，爱德华·丹多这个故事提醒了我们，在这个世界上有那么多东西值得我们去大快朵颐，你知道吗？我喜欢生活在现代世界。我太爱它了。我喜欢那种别人假装讨厌的现代食品。我吃像 Rustlers 汉堡这样的东西。你知道为什么吗？因为我喜欢。我是真的非常喜欢。人们会说，你在说什么呢？你喜欢什么？所有这一切。但你知道我的意思吗？比如喝酒，我太喜欢了。甚至，比如坐着。

00:12:45.430 --> 00:12:47.710
<v John Tothill>[LAUGHTER]
<v John Tothill>[笑声]

00:12:47.710 --> 00:12:54.510
<v John Tothill>Oh, I love it. I really love it. The irony that I am a standup comedian--
<v John Tothill>哦，我太喜欢了。我真的太喜欢了。具有讽刺意味的是，我竟然是一个单口喜剧演员——

00:12:54.510 --> 00:12:56.670
<v John Tothill>[LAUGHTER]
<v John Tothill>[笑声]

00:12:56.670 --> 00:13:35.490
<v John Tothill>--it's the one hour of the day I'm not sat on my ass. I love it. I really, really love it. I feel that very strongly. It's interesting to me that greed and gluttony are separated in religious teaching. I don't think that gluttony belongs on that list of seven deadly sins. I don't think so. I think greed does. But I think gluttony is different. I think that gluttony is something of a virtue, and I think it should be celebrated. But they are different, aren't they? We don't live in an age of gluttony, not really. Gone are the days of Edward Dando. Gone are the days of the medieval feudal lord who throws feasts and bakes pies that are birds within birds within birds and that kind of thing. It's over.
<v John Tothill>——这是我一天中唯一一小时屁股不粘凳子的时间。我太喜欢了。我真的、真的很喜欢。对此我有着非常强烈的感觉。在我看来，宗教教义将贪婪（greed）和饕餮（gluttony）区分开来，这很有趣。我不认为饕餮应该列在那七大罪的名单里。我不这么认为。我认为贪婪应该算。但我认为饕餮是不同的。我认为饕餮某种程度上算是一种美德，而且我认为它应该受到赞美。但它们是不同的，对吧？我们并没有生活在一个崇尚饕餮的时代，并非如此。爱德华·丹多的时代已经一去不复返了。中世纪封建领主的时代也已经一去不复返了，他们举办盛宴，烤出鸟里套鸟、鸟里又套鸟之类的馅饼。那些都结束了。

00:13:35.490 --> 00:13:58.150
<v John Tothill>Nowadays, the modern-day equivalent of a feudal lord is just anyone in a friendship group who owns a printer, you know, tragic. The stranglehold they have over the rest of us-- it's unbelievable. But we don't live in an age of gluttony. We live in an age of appalling greed. We live in an age of the kind of cruel, scheming, evil, greed. But it's the kind of greed which, up close, looks very moderate.
<v John Tothill>如今，现代版的封建领主不过是朋友圈里任何一个拥有打印机的人，你知道的，这真可悲。他们对我们其余人的这种钳制——简直让人难以置信。但我们并没有生活在一个饕餮的时代。我们生活在一个贪婪得可怕的时代。我们生活在一个残酷、诡计多端、邪恶的贪婪时代。但这种贪婪，如果近距离看的话，又显得非常克制。

00:13:58.150 --> 00:14:22.450
<v John Tothill>Like, it's no surprise to me our modern-day Silicon Valley overlords-- they're not gangrenous Henry VIIIs, are they, at all? They're gym bros, you know? Mark Zuckerberg is a gym bro. That guy, Bryan Johnson or whatever, he's a gym bro. Jeff Bezos is a bit of a gym bro. They take their vitamins. They take their supplements. They optimize their daily routine. They go to the gym. They cut the small talk. They want to expand their territories. They want to live forever.
<v John Tothill>就像，我对我们现代硅谷的霸主们并不感到意外——他们一点也不是亨利八世那种病态肥胖的暴君，对吧？他们是健身狂，你知道的？马克·扎克伯格是个健身狂。那个叫布赖恩·约翰逊（Bryan Johnson）还是什么的家伙，是个健身狂。杰夫·贝佐斯也算是个健身狂。他们吃维生素，吃补充剂，优化日常作息，去健身房，不废话，想要扩大领地，还想要长生不老。

00:14:22.450 --> 00:14:53.990
<v John Tothill>It's terribly egotistical, greedy behavior, whereas at least gluttony, it's not selfless. But it's humble, isn't it? Like, do you think Edward Dando liked going to prison? Of course not. But Edward Dando had the humility to say, I don't matter, my life doesn't matter as much as the celebration of these oysters, the celebration of life. In a similar way, I promise you, my bank balance does not matter as much as the celebration of a Domino's pizza.
<v John Tothill>这种行为极其自私、贪婪，而暴食至少不是无私的。但它很卑微，对吧？比如，你觉得爱德华·丹多喜欢坐牢吗？当然不喜欢。但爱德华·丹多有那种谦卑，他说，我并不重要，我的生命远没有庆祝这些牡蛎、庆祝生命来得重要。同理，我向你们保证，我的银行存款绝对没有庆祝达美乐披萨来得重要。

00:14:53.990 --> 00:15:19.050
<v John Tothill>Do you know what I mean, though? I'm trying to make the point that gluttony, it's over. The greedy men have won. Oh, it drives me mad. It does. Because I'm flying the gluttony flag out here. I believe in gluttony. And I took gluttony to a whole new level last year-- shocking. So, OK, last year, to fund my Edinburgh Fringe run, I went on a clinical trial, right? So I went on this trial, where they gave me malaria. This is true. And--
<v John Tothill>不过你们懂我的意思吗？我想表达的是，暴食已经结束了。贪婪的人赢了。哦，这把我气疯了。真是这样。因为我一直在高举暴食的旗帜。我相信暴食。我去年把暴食提升到了一个全新的高度——太惊人了。所以，好吧，去年为了筹集我去爱丁堡艺穗节演出的资金，我参加了一个临床试验，对吧？我参加了这个试验，他们让我感染了疟疾。这是真的。而且——

00:15:19.050 --> 00:15:20.390
<v John Tothill>[LAUGHTER]
<v John Tothill>[笑声]

00:15:20.390 --> 00:15:48.040
<v John Tothill>Never got a laugh before. No, but there's something for everyone on this show. No, seriously, I promise you. I went on this clinical trial. They gave me malaria. And actually, it got a bit out of hand. It resulted in a sort of touch and-- it was a near-death experience, right, this thing. I bring this up because I really felt like last year, after everything that happened, I felt like I really earned my time at the Edinburgh Fringe. I was hungry for life. I wanted to do my show. I wanted to watch other people's shows. I wanted to hang out. I was having a great time.
<v John Tothill>我以前从没逗笑过别人。不，不过这个节目里人人都能找到乐子。不，说真的，我向你们保证。我参加了这个临床试验。他们让我感染了疟疾。实际上，情况有点失控了。导致了一种差一点……这是一次濒死体验，对吧，这事。我提起这个是因为我真的觉得去年经历了这一切之后，我觉得自己真的配得上在爱丁堡艺穗节的时光。我对生活充满渴望。我想表演我的节目。我想看别人的节目。我想四处闲逛。我当时过得棒极了。

00:15:48.040 --> 00:16:23.040
<v John Tothill>And in the spirit of triumph, on about day five of the Edinburgh Fringe, I thought to myself, what would Edward Dando do? And I got myself the modern-day equivalent of oysters. I got myself some cheesy chips. I love cheesy chips. And I ate these cheesy chips slightly too close to going to bed. And I woke up-- unsurprisingly, I woke up in the most extraordinary pain. It was this horrible, dreadful stomachache. It was like someone was lying on top of me, which they were. Got them off. And then--
<v John Tothill>带着这种胜利的精神，在大约爱丁堡艺穗节的第五天，我心想，爱德华·丹多会怎么做？于是我给自己搞了份现代版的“牡蛎”。我给自己买了一些芝士薯条。我超爱芝士薯条。我吃这些芝士薯条的时间离睡觉太近了。然后我醒了——毫不意外，我醒来时感到了极其剧烈的疼痛。那是一种可怕、糟糕的胃痛。就像有人压在我身上一样，事实上也确实如此。把他们弄开。然后——

00:16:23.040 --> 00:16:25.220
<v John Tothill>[LAUGHTER]
<v John Tothill>[笑声]

00:16:25.220 --> 00:16:53.120
<v John Tothill>But it was awful-- stomachache, kind of nasty headache, a fever, it was. It was dreadful. I just remember lying there in bed and thinking, OK, I just need to get myself to the bathroom, and I will eventually pass these cheesy chips. And I'm in a lot of pain. And actually, I phone 111. And they said, how bad is the pain? And I said, it's pretty bad. It's not as bad as malaria, right?
<v John Tothill>但那感觉太糟了——胃痛，还有点难受的头痛，发烧，确实如此。太可怕了。我只记得躺在床上想，好吧，我只需要去趟洗手间，这些芝士薯条最终就会排出来的。而且我疼得很厉害。实际上，我拨打了111求助热线。他们问，有多疼？我说，挺疼的。不过没疟疾那么疼，对吧？

00:16:53.120 --> 00:16:55.020
<v John Tothill>[LAUGHTER]
<v John Tothill>[笑声]

00:16:55.020 --> 00:17:20.620
<v John Tothill>They said, what an incredibly strange thing to say. And they said, look, if it's manageable, the chances are you've probably got food poisoning. I thought, fine, food poisoning. I don't need to cancel any shows because of food poisoning. I've had malaria. I've had a proper disease. I've been looking forward to the Edinburgh Fringe for months and months and months. I really want to do my show. I'm not going to cancel a show because of food poisoning, for God's sake. So I went on stage.
<v John Tothill>他们说，这话听起来可真够奇怪的。他们说，听着，如果还能忍受，你大概是食物中毒了。我想，好吧，食物中毒。我不需要因为食物中毒取消任何演出。我得过疟疾。我得过真正的疾病。我盼望爱丁堡艺穗节已经盼了好几个月了。我真的很想演我的节目。看在上帝的份上，我才不会因为食物中毒取消演出呢。所以我上台了。

00:17:20.620 --> 00:17:43.580
<v John Tothill>But over the next few days, as I was doing these performances, basically, my body started to shut down, basically. So I would sleep for hours. I'd sleep for 18 hours or whatever. And I'd wake up. I'd take all available pain relief. I'd go and do the show. Straight afterwards, I'd collapse, and I'd sleep for another, like, 18, 19, 20 hours.
<v John Tothill>但在接下来的几天里，当我进行这些表演时，基本上，我的身体开始崩溃了。所以我一睡就是几个小时。我会睡上18个小时左右。然后我醒来。我服用所有能找到的止痛药。然后去演节目。演出一结束，我就瘫倒了，接着又睡了大约18、19、20个小时。

00:17:43.580 --> 00:18:07.100
<v John Tothill>About five days into the illness, I remember phoning up my mom and saying, this is really bad now. This is the worst pain I've ever been in. She said, how bad is it, actually? And I remember saying to my mom, I feel like I want to die. I feel like I want to be dead. And she said, you said that two weeks ago when you lost your phone. And I did.
<v John Tothill>生病大约五天后，我记得我给妈妈打电话说，现在真的很严重了。这是我经历过最剧烈的疼痛。她问，到底有多疼？我记得我对妈妈说，我觉得我想死。我觉得我真想死。她说，两周前你弄丢手机时你也这么说过。我还真说过。

00:18:07.100 --> 00:18:08.860
<v John Tothill>[LAUGHTER]
<v John Tothill>[笑声]

00:18:08.860 --> 00:18:33.630
<v John Tothill>So I'm not a reliable narrator of any of this next bit, OK? Take this with a pinch of salt. Now, I went to this pharmacy to get more laxatives, which is a five-minute walk away. And I did that walk in 45 minutes. So I thought, OK, right, I'm going to have to go to the hospital. And hospitals are difficult environments, aren't they?
<v John Tothill>所以我接下来的这部分内容可信度不高，好吗？大家听听就好，别太当真。当时我去药店买更多的泻药，药店步行只要五分钟。我却花了45分钟才走到。所以我想，好吧，我得去医院了。医院可真是个让人头疼的环境，对吧？

00:18:33.630 --> 00:18:42.550
<v John Tothill>Because, also, the hospital as an adult is a place where you have to kind of advocate for yourself, right? Which basically means doing an impression of your own mom, you know?
<v John Tothill>因为成年人去医院，也得是一个你得为自己据理力争的地方，对吧？这基本上就等于模仿你自己的妈妈，你知道吗？

00:18:42.550 --> 00:18:43.730
<v John Tothill>[LAUGHTER]
<v John Tothill>[笑声]

00:18:43.730 --> 00:18:55.710
<v John Tothill>And that's adulthood, isn't it, sadly? You go around as an adult, doing sort of impressions of your own parents, until eventually, it sticks. The other day, I roasted a chicken. It was like a drag act or something.
<v John Tothill>这就是成年人的生活，不是吗，真可悲？你以成年人的身份到处走，模仿你自己的父母，直到最后，这种状态成了你的常态。前几天，我烤了一只鸡。那感觉就像一场变装秀什么的。

00:18:55.710 --> 00:18:57.010
<v John Tothill>[LAUGHTER]
<v John Tothill>[笑声]

00:18:57.010 --> 00:19:19.210
<v John Tothill>Fuck's going on here? Am I in the opening number of a musical or something? Bizarre. So I went into this A&E waiting room. And eventually, they said, right, the doctor will see you now. And this doctor, she took me into a curtained-off area. And she said, look. She said, I don't know what's wrong with you, but-- she said, the good news is, with things like abdominal pain, she said, you've actually left it a very long time to come in. I'd left it seven or eight days, something like that.
<v John Tothill>这到底是怎么回事？我是走进了音乐剧的开场曲还是怎么着？太诡异了。于是我走进了急诊室的候诊区。最后，他们说，好了，医生现在可以见你了。这位女医生把我带进了一个用帘子隔开的区域。她看着我说，听着。她说，我不知道你出了什么毛病，但是——她说，好消息是，像腹痛这种症状，她说，你其实拖了很久才来看病。我拖了七八天，大概这么久。

00:19:19.210 --> 00:19:43.530
<v John Tothill>So she said, because you've left it so long, it's unlikely to be anything that serious. With these abdominal things, if it's something like-- if it's appendicitis or if it's a perforated bowel or something like that, she said, you'd have been dead by now. So she said, actually, the fact that you're still here means it's probably quite mild. It'll be an infection. We'll figure out what it is. We'll give you some tablets, and you can go home. You can probably do your show tonight, if not tomorrow. Great. Fine.
<v John Tothill>所以她说，因为你拖了这么久，太严重的情况可能性不大了。对于这些腹部问题，如果是某种情况——如果是阑尾炎、肠穿孔之类的话，她说，你现在早就死了。所以她说，实际上，你还能坐在这里，说明病情可能相当轻微。应该只是感染。我们会查出是什么问题。给你开点药，你就能回家了。今天晚上甚至明天你大概就能演出了。太好了。行吧。

00:19:43.530 --> 00:20:04.330
<v John Tothill>Took me for a load of scans, figure out what was going on. And she came back with quite a confused, quite grave look on her face. And she said, OK, you're not going to believe this, but it is appendicitis. And I said, oh, isn't that funny? And she said, no.
<v John Tothill>她带我去做了一堆扫描，查明到底是怎么回事。等她回来时，脸上带着困惑而严峻的表情。她说，好吧，你可能不会相信，但这就是阑尾炎。我说，哦，这不是很滑稽吗？她说，才不滑稽。

00:20:04.330 --> 00:20:10.210
<v John Tothill>[LAUGHTER]
<v John Tothill>[笑声]

00:20:10.210 --> 00:20:32.320
<v John Tothill>In her words, she said, your appendix has not just become infected. She said it hasn't even just ruptured, which is the usual sort of worst-case scenario for appendicitis. She said, your appendix ruptured days ago and your appendix has now-- in her words-- obliterated. She said, your appendix has completely fallen apart, and it is now floating around your body.
<v John Tothill>用她的话说，她说，你的阑尾不仅是感染了。她说甚至不仅是破裂了，而那是阑尾炎最典型的最坏情况。她说，你的阑尾几天前就破裂了，而且现在——用她的原话说——已经粉碎了。她说，你的阑尾已经彻底碎掉了，现在正漂浮在你的腹腔里。

00:20:32.320 --> 00:20:47.560
<v John Tothill>She said, you are in a state called intra-abdominal sepsis. And she said, we need to operate on you in the next few hours or you're going to die. She said, so we need to get you to the theater right now. I said, that's what I've been trying to tell you.
<v John Tothill>她说，你正处于一种叫做腹腔内脓毒症的状态中。她说，我们必须在接下来的几个小时内给你做手术，否则你会死的。她说，所以我们得马上把你送进手术室。我说，这正是我一直想告诉你们的。

00:20:47.560 --> 00:20:50.520
<v John Tothill>[LAUGHTER]
<v John Tothill>[笑声]

00:20:50.520 --> 00:21:15.800
<v John Tothill>Get me to the theater. But look, I'm not going to pretend it wasn't scary, because obviously, it was scary. And because suddenly, it was a real-life emergency. They put me in this hospital bed, and they bumped me up the urgent surgery list and stuff. And I woke up from the surgery. And the surgeon said-- this is an exact quote. He said, "Yeah, when we opened you up, we were all a bit like, yikes."
<v John Tothill>把我送进手术室吧。不过听着，我不会假装这不让人害怕，因为它确实很吓人。因为这突然变成了一场现实生活中的紧急医疗状况。他们把我安置在病床上，把我提升到紧急手术名单的前列等等。我从手术中醒来。外科医生说——这是原话。他说：“说实话，当我们剖开你的时候，我们所有人都觉得：哎哟。”

00:21:15.800 --> 00:21:21.240
<v John Tothill>[LAUGHTER]
<v John Tothill>[笑声]

00:21:21.240 --> 00:21:50.380
<v John Tothill>Now, they don't know what causes appendicitis, and it's been suggested that excessively fatty diets might lead to appendicitis. I was essentially almost killed by my own gluttony. And then he told me off, basically. He got cross with me. And he said, in my 25-year career as a general surgeon, he said, I've never seen someone come in that late with appendicitis because of this kind of dogged attitude I had to doing the show every day. And I didn't want to cancel it.
<v John Tothill>现在，医学界还不知道是什么引发了阑尾炎，有人提出过分油腻的饮食可能会导致阑尾炎。我基本上差点被自己的贪食给害死。然后他把我训了一顿。他生我的气，他说，在他25年的普通外科医生生涯中，他说，他从来没见过有人因为这种每天坚持演出的顽固态度，把阑尾炎拖到这么晚才来看病的。而且我当时还不想取消演出。

00:21:50.380 --> 00:22:10.460
<v John Tothill>And he said that was dangerous, and it was stupid, and it was reckless, and it was immature. And he said, if you'd have left it one day more, you'd be dead. You wouldn't be here. And that'd be it. And it's really, really hard to hear that and not think he's saying that I came in at exactly the right time.
<v John Tothill>他说这很危险，很愚蠢，很鲁莽，而且很不成熟。他说，如果你再拖上一天，你今天就死定了。你就不会坐在这里了。事情就会这样结束。听到这话，你真的很难不认为他其实是在变相夸奖我来得正是时候。

00:22:10.460 --> 00:22:20.800
<v John Tothill>[LAUGHTER]
<v John Tothill>[笑声]

00:22:20.800 --> 00:22:54.600
<v John Tothill>So you're saying-- yeah, so I was right not to cancel yesterday's show. Thank you. My timing's immaculate. I was incredibly lucky. I feel incredibly lucky to survive. Anyway, right, Edward Dando. So Edward Dando died when he was my age. Edward Dando died when he was 29 years old. He was also a man of timing, actually, because he died just as oysters were starting to become overfarmed, and they were falling out of fashion in London, and oyster restaurants were starting to close and go out of business.
<v John Tothill>所以你是说——对，所以我昨天没取消演出是正确的。谢谢。我掐的时间真是天衣无缝。我真是走运极了。我觉得自己能活下来简直太幸运了。不管怎样，说回爱德华·丹多。爱德华·丹多去世时和我现在的年纪一样。爱德华·丹多去世时年仅29岁。其实他也是个很懂时机的人，因为他死的时候，牡蛎刚好开始过度养殖，在伦敦逐渐失宠，牡蛎餐厅也开始倒闭关门。

00:22:54.600 --> 00:23:28.530
<v John Tothill>And it'd be really easy to think of the story of Edward Dando as the story of a man who never learned his lesson. But I don't think that's true. I think Edward Dando did learn his lesson. I think Edward Dando resolved, every single time, to continue doing the thing that he absolutely loved more than anything else. And as I lay there in that hospital bed, anxious to come back to the Edinburgh Fringe, I thought of Edward Dando in solitary confinement. And I hope that every single time, he thought to himself, when I get out of here, do you know what I really fancy?
<v John Tothill>我们很容易把爱德华·丹多的故事看作是一个从未吸取教训的人的故事。但我不这么认为。我觉得爱德华·丹多确实吸取了教训。我认为爱德华·丹多每次都下定决心，要继续去做他绝对最热爱的事情。当我躺在医院的病床上，急切地想回到爱丁堡艺穗节时，我想到了身处单人牢房里的爱德华·丹多。我希望他每一次都在心里想：等我从这里出去，你知道我最想吃什么吗？

00:23:28.530 --> 00:23:31.430
<v John Tothill>[LAUGHTER]
<v John Tothill>[笑声]

00:23:31.430 --> 00:23:49.650
<v John Tothill>And I hope that for him, every time, it was worth the wait. Because I can promise you from the bottom of my heart, this certainly has been. I beg you, please, be an Edward Dando. Eat the cheesy chips. Drink the champagne. Refuse to go hungry in a land of plenty, even if a thing like sepsis or prison gets in your way.
<v John Tothill>我希望对他来说，每一次等待都是值得的。因为我可以发自内心地向你们保证，对我和我的经历来说，绝对是这样的。我求求你们，请务必当一个爱德华·丹多。去吃芝士薯条吧。去喝香槟吧。在一个物产丰富的国度里拒绝挨饿，哪怕有败血症或牢狱之灾阻挡你的去路。

00:23:49.650 --> 00:23:51.190
<v John Tothill>[LAUGHTER]
<v John Tothill>[笑声]

00:23:51.190 --> 00:23:54.050
<v John Tothill>And for God's sake, let's go and get some oysters. Thank you very much.
<v John Tothill>看在上帝的份上，我们去吃点牡蛎吧。非常感谢。

00:23:54.050 --> 00:24:02.630
<v John Tothill>[CHEERING]
<v John Tothill>[欢呼声]

00:24:02.630 --> 00:24:27.830
<v Ira Glass>John Tothill. I should say, the historical record is kind of sparse on Edward Dando. So John took a few liberties to fill in the gaps. He got the gist right. As to whether a fatty diet can cause appendicitis, like they say, more research is needed. John's show was directed by Alex Cartlidge and Catherine Craigmyle. He's on tour right now across the UK. You can find details on his Instagram, @johntothill.
<v Ira Glass>约翰·托思希尔。我得说明一下，关于爱德华·丹多的历史记载相当稀少。所以约翰发挥了一些艺术加工来填补空白。他抓住了核心要点。至于高脂肪饮食是否会导致阑尾炎，就像他们说的，还需要更多的研究。约翰的演出由亚历克斯·卡特利奇和凯瑟琳·克雷格迈尔导演。他目前正在英国各地巡演。你可以在他的Instagram上找到详细信息，账号是@johntothill。

00:24:27.830 --> 00:24:37.470
<v Ira Glass>Producer Aviva DeKornfeld from our show helped him adapt this story for our program.
<v Ira Glass>我们节目的制作人阿维瓦·德科恩菲尔德协助他将这个故事改编成了我们的节目。

00:24:37.470 --> 00:24:40.510
<v Ira Glass>"Act Two, The Joy of Ex."
<v Ira Glass>第二幕，“前任的快乐”。

00:24:40.510 --> 00:24:55.790
<v Ira Glass>OK. So sometimes, you break up with somebody, and you still talk. You're friendly. Some exes, you never speak with again. It's harsh, but everybody understands it when somebody's feelings are crushed. It's not unusual.
<v Ira Glass>好的。有时候，你和某人分手了，但你们依然会聊天。你们还是朋友。而对于另一些前任，你再也不会和他们说话。这很残酷，但如果一方的心碎了，大家都能理解。这并不罕见。

00:24:55.790 --> 00:25:17.550
<v Ira Glass>What is unusual is somebody who wants to be in touch with all their exes-- I mean, all of them, even the ones who never want to see his face again, the ones who feel really hurt and disappointed. Evan Roberts is a person who wants to reach out like that and become friends with the most hostile of his exes. It's a weird mission that one of our producers, Tobin Low, was curious about.
<v Ira Glass>罕见的是那种想和所有前任都保持联系的人——我是说所有人，甚至包括那些再也不想看到他的脸、那些感到备受伤害和失望的人。埃文·罗伯茨就是这样一个人，他想要去接触并试图和那些对他最充满敌意的旧爱成为朋友。这是一个奇怪的使命，我们的制作人托宾·洛对此非常好奇。

00:25:17.550 --> 00:25:46.030
<v Tobin Low>Evan made a list of 17 exes. By the time he and I met, he had talked with 16 of them over the last several years. It was nice to catch up, get their side of the story. But the more the thing had gone on, the more he realized this whole mission was actually just about ex number 17, someone he referred to as The Big Ex, a guy named Keith who, even when Evan reached out, did not want to talk. They'd broken up around New Year's. It was 2010. A couple of weeks after--
<v Tobin Low>埃文列出了一份包含17位前任的名单。当我和他见面时，在过去的几年里，他已经和其中的16个人交谈过了。叙叙旧、听听他们对那段感情的看法，感觉挺不错的。但随着这件事的进行，他越来越意识到，这个宏大的使命其实全都是为了第17位前任，也就是他口中的“终极大前任”——一个名叫基恩的男人。即使在埃文主动联系时，基恩也完全不想说话。他们大约在新年前后分手。那是2010年。几周之后——

00:25:46.030 --> 00:26:20.580
<v Evan Roberts>I was on my bike, and I was going through the little atrium of the apartment building and getting the mail. And I got this card from Keith. And Keith repurposes cards and envelopes and puts stickers on them and stuff. So I opened it up at the mail-- like, I'm blocking the door. I'm sure I opened it up at the mailbox. And on the cover is a winter scene. And there's some blue dotted stickers he has on it. And where the sentiment of the card should have been was just a ripped-out hole.
<v Evan Roberts>我当时正骑着自行车，穿过公寓楼的小中庭去取信。我收到了基恩寄来的一张卡片。基恩喜欢把卡片和信封重新改造，在上面贴满贴纸什么的。所以我当时就在邮箱旁边打开了它——就像这样，我挡住了门。我确信我是在邮箱旁边打开的。卡片封面是一个冬日场景。上面贴着一些他弄的蓝色圆点贴纸。而本该写着祝福语的地方，却被撕出了一个洞。

00:26:20.580 --> 00:26:24.980
<v Tobin Low>Keith had torn out the part where there should have been "Merry Christmas" or "Happy holidays."
<v Tobin Low>基恩撕掉了本该写着“圣诞快乐”或“节日快乐”的那部分。

00:26:24.980 --> 00:26:32.940
<v Evan Roberts>It was a void. And I saw through the hole onto my shoe on the floor.
<v Evan Roberts>那是一个空洞。透过那个洞，我看到了自己踩在地上的鞋子。

00:26:32.940 --> 00:26:50.580
<v Tobin Low>On the opposite flap, Keith wrote a note about how he thought he and Evan should stay away from each other. I think most people receiving a holiday card with the heartfelt sentiment surgically removed would think, message received. I will also cut you out of my life, ex-lover. That would be my response, anyway.
<v Tobin Low>在对面的折页上，基恩写了一段话，大意是他觉得他和埃文应该保持距离。我想大多数人收到一张饱含深情的祝福语被“外科手术般”移除的节日卡片时，都会觉得：信息已收到。我也要把你从我的生活中删除了，前任。无论如何，这会是我的反应。

00:26:50.580 --> 00:27:08.100
<v Tobin Low>I will be honest, the first time I heard about the fact that you had interviewed all of these exes, I was horrified by that. I'm such a-- I don't talk to any of my exes, not out of ill will or anything. But it's not a thing I've ever done.
<v Tobin Low>说实话，当我第一次听说你采访了所有这些前任时，我感到很震惊。我是一个……我从来不和任何前任说话，倒不是因为有什么恶意或者别的什么。但这真不是我做过的事。

00:27:08.100 --> 00:27:15.500
<v Evan Roberts>Why don't you keep in touch with them? Is it just done? It's like it's toast and you can't make toast bread?
<v Evan Roberts>你为什么不和他们保持联系呢？这就彻底结束了吗？就像烤成了面包干就没法再变回面包了一样？

00:27:15.500 --> 00:27:37.500
<v Tobin Low>I feel like the relationship we had exists very much in the time that we were together. And so to revisit it to me feels like going to a past life. I don't know the value of that, I guess, for me, personally.
<v Tobin Low>我觉得我们曾经拥有的关系，很大程度上只存在于我们在一起的那段时光里。所以重温它对我来说感觉就像是去了前世。我个人觉得，这对我来说没什么价值。

00:27:37.500 --> 00:27:55.820
<v Evan Roberts>I would challenge you, I guess. There are so many other ways that it could be, and this sort of dead version is like the least interesting. And what I realized through some of these interviews is if there was love there once, it's kind of always there for me.
<v Evan Roberts>我想对你发起挑战。事情其实有很多种不同的发展可能，而这种死气沉沉的所谓“结束”反而是最没意思的。通过其中一些采访我意识到的是，如果曾经那里有过爱，对我来说，这种爱某种程度上就永远都在。

00:27:55.820 --> 00:28:23.060
<v Tobin Low>Just to say, Evan has been in a happy relationship with his boyfriend for over a decade. But ever since he and Keith broke up 16 years ago, he's wondered, why the card? Why did Keith never want to talk to him again? Why not be friends? Had he missed something when they broke up, done something bad without realizing it? Evan and Keith met in their early 30s. They dated long distance. Evan lived in San Francisco, Keith in Austin.
<v Tobin Low>顺便说一下，埃文和他的男友一直保持着幸福的恋爱关系，已经走过十多年了。但自从他与基恩16年前分手以来，他一直百思不得其解：为什么要寄那张卡片？为什么基恩再也不想和他说话？为什么不能做朋友？难道他们在分手时漏掉了什么，或者在不知情的情况下做了什么糟糕的事？埃文和基恩在三十岁出头时相遇。他们维持了一段异地恋。埃文住在旧金山，基恩住在奥斯汀。

00:28:23.060 --> 00:28:46.340
<v Tobin Low>Evan loved who he was with Keith, said he felt like himself at a higher octave. Keith helped him apply to film school, which he'd always dreamed about. Keith was outgoing, funny. One time when Keith was visiting Evan, Evan came home to find him fake passed out on the living room with a message spelled out in matchsticks. T-T-O-Y-M-M-H. The Thought Of You Makes Me Hot.
<v Tobin Low>埃文深爱着和基恩在一起时的自己，他说那种感觉就像是处于高八度的自我状态中。基恩帮他申请了电影学院，那是他一直以来的梦想。基恩外向、风趣。有一次基恩去探望埃文，埃文回到家，发现基恩假装晕倒在客厅的地上，用火柴棍拼出了一行字：T-T-O-Y-M-M-H（The Thought Of You Makes Me Hot，想起你就让我燥热难耐）。

00:28:46.340 --> 00:29:09.380
<v Tobin Low>But long distance was hard on them. They broke up a couple of times, would always get back together. They were on one of those breaks when Keith moved to San Francisco, said, look, we could make a real go of this now. Let's make this work. Evan was surprised, taken aback. And instead of jumping in feet first, he said, OK, but let's take our time, not rush things, which Keith did not take well.
<v Tobin Low>但异地恋对他们来说很难。他们分过几次手，但总是会复合。基斯搬到旧金山时，他们正处于其中一次分手中，基斯说，听着，我们现在可以真正试着发展一下了。让我们把这事成全了吧。埃文很惊讶，有点不知所措。他没有一头扎进去，而是说，好吧，但我们慢慢来，别着急，而基斯对此并不买账。

00:29:09.380 --> 00:29:20.210
<v Evan Roberts>His face changed. Something about his whole body changed. And it did feel like a door closing.
<v Evan Roberts>他的脸变了。他全身的某种东西变了。那种感觉确实像一扇门在关上。

00:29:20.210 --> 00:29:23.690
<v Tobin Low>And did that surprise you?
<v Tobin Low>这让你感到意外吗？

00:29:23.690 --> 00:29:27.990
<v Evan Roberts>Only because I didn't know that what I said would have that impact.
<v Evan Roberts>只是因为我不知道我说的话会产生那样的影响。

00:29:27.990 --> 00:29:34.650
<v Tobin Low>Keith broke up with Evan for good. Afterwards, Evan felt like he was walking around in a fog.
<v Tobin Low>基斯彻底和埃文分手了。之后，埃文觉得自己像是在雾中行走。

00:29:34.650 --> 00:29:43.610
<v Evan Roberts>It felt like I was stuck on the wrong timeline. I just felt in a different parallel universe that I wasn't supposed to be in.
<v Evan Roberts>感觉就像我被困在了错误的时间线上。我只是觉得自己身处一个本不该属于我的不同平行宇宙中。

00:29:43.610 --> 00:30:01.570
<v Tobin Low>Almost immediately, Keith got into a different very serious relationship, which has only further puzzled Evan. They both moved on. Why not become friends now? But Evan's reached out a couple times over the years, and Keith has always been chilly. It's vexed Evan so long, it's become a joke with his friends.
<v Tobin Low>几乎立刻，基斯就展开了另一段非常认真的恋爱关系，这让埃文更加困惑。两人都向前看了。为什么现在不能做朋友呢？但这些年来埃文主动联系过好几次，基斯却一直很冷淡。这困扰了埃文很久，甚至成了他和朋友们的一个笑话。

00:30:01.570 --> 00:30:20.050
<v Tobin Low>One of them was in Keith's neighborhood recently and texted Evan about it. His friend joked that he should go take a selfie in front of Keith's house. Evan looked up Keith's address on his computer, copied and pasted it into a text to his friend, hit Send. Then he realized he had accidentally texted Keith's address to Keith.
<v Tobin Low>其中一人最近在基斯的社区附近，并就此给埃文发了短信。他的朋友开玩笑说，他应该去基斯家门前自拍一张。埃文在电脑上查了基斯的地址，复制并粘贴到发给朋友的短信中，点击了发送。然后他意识到，他不小心把基斯的地址发给了基斯。

00:30:20.050 --> 00:30:36.570
<v Tobin Low>I screamed when Evan told me this. He thinks he saved it by quickly editing the text to a period, pretending it was an accidental butt text. Did you know can edit texts on an iPhone? I didn't. Recently, he wrote to Keith one last time, asking if he would talk.
<v Tobin Low>埃文告诉我这件事时，我尖叫了。他以为自己通过迅速将短信编辑为一个句号来掩饰，假装那是不小心按错发的短信。你知道iPhone可以编辑短信吗？我不知道。最近，他最后一次写信给基斯，问他愿不愿意聊聊。

00:30:36.570 --> 00:30:46.890
<v Evan Roberts>I see the email. Keith responded. [GASPS] Oh my god, yes, count me in.
<v Evan Roberts>我看到了那封电子邮件。基斯回复了。[倒吸气] 天哪，太好了，算我一个。

